    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Building Piecewise Linear Signals",
    "lesson_core_move": "Construct a piecewise linear continuous-time signal by converting each breakpoint's slope change into a shifted ramp term gated by unit steps.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Building Piecewise Linear Signals",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Building Piecewise Linear Signals",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Your job in this step is to represent piecewise linear signals with reusable building blocks, not by writing many separate case lines. A slope change at time $a$ becomes one shifted ramp term, so many breakpoints can be combined into a single expression.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the shifted unit-ramp definition. The block $r(t-a)$ is a ramp that starts at $a$, and multiplying by $u(t-a)$ gates it on after that time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "r(t-a)= (t-a)u(t-a)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the base case: $x(t)=m r(t-a)=m(t-a)u(t-a)$. It is zero before $a$, then grows linearly with slope $m$ after $a$, which gives the exact same behavior as the simplest slope segment.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The core move is to add slope increments. If $\\Delta m_1$ appears at $a_1$, it contributes only for $t \\ge a_1$; if $\\Delta m_2$ appears at $a_2$, it contributes only for $t \\ge a_2$, etc. At any $t$, active slope is the sum of these contributions.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=m_0 r(t-a_0)+\\Delta m_1\\big[r(t-a_1)-r(t-a_2)\\big]+\\Delta m_2\\big[r(t-a_2)-r(t-a_3)\\big]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A difference of shifted ramps creates a finite-interval slope block. The term $\\Delta m [r(t-a)-r(t-b)]$ adds slope $\\Delta m$ from $a$ until $b$, and then stops changing afterward. This is the key pattern for segmenting piecewise linear segments cleanly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Next, you can generalize this recipe from one ramp to one breakpoint increment, then to a finite interval, and then to several increments chained together in the full signal form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Building Piecewise Linear Signals",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Starting a Signal with One Shifted Ramp",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Express $x(t)$ where $x(t)=0$ for $t<-1$ and $x(t)=2(t+1)$ for $t\\ge-1$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the cue from the prompt: the signal is flat at $0$ before the start time and becomes linear with slope $2$ at $t=-1$, which means this is one shifted ramp segment.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The target is to express the turn-on time with a ramp shifted to $-1$. For a ramp shifted by $a$, the rule is $\\Delta m r(t-a)=\\Delta m(t-a)u(t-a)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "r(t-a)=(t-a)u(t-a)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the rule with $a=-1$ and slope $\\Delta m=2$: first form the shifted ramp, then scale it by $2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2r(t+1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2(t+1)u(t+1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Check the two regions: for $t<-1$, $u(t+1)=0$, so $x(t)=0$; for $t\\ge-1$, $u(t+1)=1$, so $x(t)=2(t+1)$. This matches the prompt exactly and is the compact one-ramp model for this step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Building Piecewise Linear Signals",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Adding a Slope Change at a Breakpoint",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Express a signal that has slope $0$ for $t<0$, slope $1$ on $[0,3)$, and slope $4$ for $t\\ge3$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the signal does not just turn on once; its slope changes at the breakpoint $t=3$. Start with the slope-$1$ ramp that begins at $t=0$, then add only the extra slope needed after $t=3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "r(t-a)=(t-a)u(t-a)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The first active segment has slope $1$, so the baseline term is $r(t)$. At the breakpoint, the slope must increase from $1$ to $4$, so the slope increment is $\\Delta m=4-1=3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Delta m=4-1=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A slope increment at $t=3$ is represented by a shifted ramp increment, so add $3r(t-3)$ to the original ramp.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=r(t)+3r(t-3)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=t u(t)+3(t-3)u(t-3)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Check the intervals by reading active ramps. For $t<0$, neither ramp is active, so the slope is $0$. For $0\\le t<3$, only $r(t)$ is active, so the slope is $1$. For $t\\ge3$, both terms are active, so the slope is $1+3=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Building Piecewise Linear Signals",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Returning the Slope to Zero After an Interval",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Construct $x(t)$ where $x(t)=2t$ for $0 \\le t < 2$ and constant for $t \\ge 2$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the slope should stay at $2$ only on $[0,2)$ and then stop changing. That makes this a finite-interval slope block, so the right move is to start with the active ramp and then cancel it with a delayed copy.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "r(t)=t u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the slope-$2$ ramp $2r(t)$, which turns on at $t=0$. To return the slope to zero at $t=2$, subtract the same ramp shifted right by $2$, $2r(t-2)$, so the added slope disappears after the interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2r(t)-2r(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2t u(t)-2(t-2)u(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Check the regions: for $t<0$, both ramps are off, so $x(t)=0$; for $0 \\le t < 2$, only the first ramp is active, so $x(t)=2t$; for $t \\ge 2$, both ramps are active, so the slope terms cancel and the signal stays constant at $4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Building Piecewise Linear Signals",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Combining Multiple Slope Changes in One Expression",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Represent a signal with slope profile: $0$ for $t<0$, $1$ for $0 \\le t<2$, $4$ for $2 \\le t<5$, and $2$ for $t\\ge5$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a slope profile with three changes: the slope turns on at $t=0$, jumps again at $t=2$, and then drops at $t=5$. Read each change as one shifted-ramp term, and add the terms in time order.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the baseline slope-$1$ ramp $r(t)$. The change at $t=2$ is $+3$, because the slope goes from $1$ to $4$, and the change at $t=5$ is $-2$, because the slope goes from $4$ to $2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=r(t)+3r(t-2)-2r(t-5)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=t u(t)+3(t-2)u(t-2)-2(t-5)u(t-5)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Check the intervals: for $t<0$, all ramps are off, so the slope is $0$; for $0 \\le t<2$, only $r(t)$ is active, so the slope is $1$; for $2 \\le t<5$, the first two ramps are active, so the slope is $1+3=4$; for $t\\ge5$, all three ramps are active, so the slope is $1+3-2=2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
